import{_ as t,o as r,c as a,ao as d}from"./chunks/framework.8Wkhwg0B.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Dependencies","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"dependencies.md","filePath":"dependencies.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"dependencies.md"};function n(o,e,s,l,c,h){return r(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="Dependencies" tabindex="-1">Dependencies <a class="header-anchor" href="#Dependencies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dependencies {#Dependencies}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every dependency of QuantumEngine is resolved in a fresh environment before it is added, and the manifest is checked for <code>_jll</code> packages outside the standard libraries and for packages with an <code>Artifacts.toml</code>. The test item &quot;core closure is binary-free&quot; repeats the check for the whole package.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">dependency</th><th style="text-align:right;">compat</th><th style="text-align:right;">resolved closure (outside the standard libraries)</th><th style="text-align:right;">standard libraries</th><th style="text-align:right;"><code>_jll</code> / artifacts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">StrictMode</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>0.5.21</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">StrictMode 0.5.22, PrecompileTools 1.4.0, Preferences 1.6.1, TypeContracts 0.14.7</td><td style="text-align:right;">Base64, Dates, InteractiveUtils, JuliaSyntaxHighlighting, Markdown, Printf, Serialization, StyledStrings, TOML, Unicode</td><td style="text-align:right;">none</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">StaticArrays</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1.9</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">StaticArrays 1.9.25, StaticArraysCore 1.4.5, PrecompileTools 1.4.0, Preferences 1.6.1</td><td style="text-align:right;">Artifacts, Dates, Libdl, LinearAlgebra, Printf, Random, SHA, TOML, Unicode; the standard-library <code>_jll</code> packages CompilerSupportLibraries_jll, OpenBLAS_jll and libblastrampoline_jll (through LinearAlgebra)</td><td style="text-align:right;">none outside the standard libraries</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LinearAlgebra</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1.13</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">none: a standard library, already in the closure through StaticArrays</td><td style="text-align:right;">Artifacts, Libdl; the standard-library <code>_jll</code> packages CompilerSupportLibraries_jll, OpenBLAS_jll and libblastrampoline_jll</td><td style="text-align:right;">none outside the standard libraries</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Printf</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1.11</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">none: a standard library (version 1.11.0 in Julia 1.13), already in the closure through StrictMode and StaticArrays</td><td style="text-align:right;">Unicode</td><td style="text-align:right;">none</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Parsers</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>3</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Parsers 3.0.6, PrecompileTools 1.4.0, Preferences 1.6.1</td><td style="text-align:right;">Dates, Printf, TOML, Unicode</td><td style="text-align:right;">none</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">JSON</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1.6</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">at JSON 1.6.1: JSON 1.6.1, Parsers 2.8.11, StructUtils 2.12.1, PrecompileTools 1.4.0, Preferences 1.6.1; at JSON 1.13.4, the newest the bound admits: JSON 1.13.4, Parsers 3.0.6, StructUtils 2.12.1, PrecompileTools 1.4.0, Preferences 1.6.1</td><td style="text-align:right;">Dates, Logging, Printf, Random, SHA, TOML, UUIDs, Unicode</td><td style="text-align:right;">none</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Parsers 3.0.6 comes from the General registry. It reads the exact-exchange fraction of a Libxc hybrid method. Its exact decimal path, the one that places a value the 64-bit paths cannot, allocates a digit buffer, so <code>method_word</code> and <code>_fraction</code> are exempt from <code>:noalloc</code>. The exemption is removed when a Parsers release contains JuliaData/Parsers.jl#244.</p><p>JSON (JSON.jl) reads JSON input. Its closure was resolved twice in fresh environments, pinned to 1.6.1 and at 1.13.4; neither has a <code>_jll</code> package outside the standard libraries or an <code>Artifacts.toml</code>. JSON 1.6 and 1.7 require Parsers 1 or 2, and QuantumEngine requires Parsers 3, so the compat bound <code>&quot;1.6&quot;</code> resolves to JSON 1.8 or later (1.13.4 here). <code>json_node</code> reads through the public <code>JSON.parse(text; dicttype = T)</code>, with an object type <code>T</code> that keeps every key in the order of the text, a repeated one included, which the conversion to <code>Node</code>s then rejects; JSON 1.6.1 and 1.13.4 both build objects through <code>T()</code> and <code>setindex!</code> alone. TrimCheck rejects <code>json_node</code> on both versions, so the trimmed program reads <code>.inp</code> input only. <code>JSON.parse</code> returns its values as <code>Any</code>, so <code>json_node</code>, <code>parse_json</code> and the helpers that read JSON.jl&#39;s values are exempt from the StrictMode guarantees; <code>parse_structured</code>, which reads only <code>Node</code>s, is held to all of them.</p><h2 id="Weak-dependencies" tabindex="-1">Weak dependencies <a class="header-anchor" href="#Weak-dependencies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Weak dependencies {#Weak-dependencies}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">weak dependency</th><th style="text-align:right;">extension</th><th style="text-align:right;">compat</th><th style="text-align:right;">resolved closure (outside the standard libraries)</th><th style="text-align:right;"><code>_jll</code> / artifacts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">YAML</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>QuantumEngineYAMLExt</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>0.4.18</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">YAML 0.4.18, StringEncodings 0.3.7, LazyJLLWrappers 1.2.7, Preferences 1.6.1, Libiconv_jll 1.18.1</td><td style="text-align:right;">Libiconv_jll 1.18.1, with an <code>Artifacts.toml</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>YAML (YAML.jl) reads YAML input files. It is a <code>[weakdeps]</code> entry, not a dependency: the extension <code>QuantumEngineYAMLExt</code> loads only when a program loads YAML.jl itself, and the package reads structured input from any <code>AbstractDict</code> without it. Its closure, resolved in a fresh environment, brings the binary library Libiconv_jll (through StringEncodings), so it stays outside the core closure. The item &quot;core closure is binary-free&quot; resolves a fresh environment holding only QuantumEngine, which installs no weak dependency, so it checks the core without the extension. A trimmed program does not load the extension.</p>',8)])])}const y=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,y as default};
